[$in title:["Wiring"]]
[$in description:["Pass a binding through a helper with Wire and choose how later input fits a message with Route."]]
[$in head:[language_theme_head]]

[navbar]
[sidebar]
listed = sync_navigation(sidebar_id)!
[section, $md:
    # Wiring

    A row editor can show a title before it knows what its user will type.
    The caller already knows which row that edit belongs to. Moth lets the
    caller express both facts in a function call. A `Wire` identifies the
    binding to read. A `Route` describes the message an edit would produce.
    For the exact argument and capture rules, see the @#wiring-reference (full Wiring reference).
]

[section:
    [doc_level:
        [$in key:wiring-introduction]
        [$in title:What a row editor needs]
        [$in advanced, $md:
            An ordinary caller can construct a rename message as soon as it knows
            the row ID and the replacement text. If those values arrive at different
            times, some part of the program has to remember how they fit together.

            Moth makes the two pieces of data explicit in parameter types.
            `Wire of String` lets a helper forward a particular title binding.
            `Route of String -> TodoMessage` identifies a choice constructor and
            records the leading values it needs. A library can accept the same Route
            shape for different callers without knowing their row IDs. Each caller
            supplies a constructor and its captured values. A closure could run
            arbitrary code when called. Here the capture expressions run when
            the caller forms the Route. The only remaining work the Route
            describes is completing the chosen variant.
        ]

        [introduction_basic]
    ]
]

[section:
    [doc_level:
        [$in key:wiring-wires]
        [$in title:Pass a title through a helper]
        [$in advanced, $md:
            The signature determines where binding identity stops. `print_title`
            can print a computed value or a literal. `show_row` can receive a
            Wire, then pass its current text to that ordinary parameter.

            [codeblock, $code "moth":
                print_title |text String| {
                    io.line(text)
                }

                show_row |title Wire of String| {
                    print_title(title)
                }

                draft ~= "Draft"
                show_row(draft)
                print_title("Untitled")
            ]

            Writing `show_row("Untitled")` would fail because the literal supplies
            text without a binding. Naming it first makes a new binding with
            its own identity. An ordinary `String` parameter inside another
            function can't recover the identity of its caller's argument.

            A Wire grants read access. To change the caller's binding, an
            ordinary mutable parameter needs an exclusive argument such as
            `replace_title(~draft)`. See @../bindings/#mutable-bindings (Mutable bindings).
        ]

        [wires_basic]
    ]
]

[section:
    [doc_level:
        [$in key:wiring-routes]
        [$in title:Choose the message at the call site]
        [$in advanced, $md:
            The same constructor expression can describe three different
            operations. Consider a window size with a default height.

            [codeblock, $code "moth":
                WindowEvent = {
                    Resized | width I32, height I32 = 720 |,
                }
            ]

            Suppose the caller writes `WindowEvent::Resized(1280)`.

            - A `WindowEvent` parameter receives the completed value. It has width 1280 and height 720.
            - A `Route of WindowEvent` parameter captures the complete payload, including the default height. It needs no further input.
            - A `Route of I32 -> WindowEvent` parameter captures width 1280 and reserves the height for the future input. The default never supplies that field.

            With one input, every field before the final one needs an explicit
            positional argument, even a field with a default. Giving a height as
            well would leave no field for the input. With zero inputs, ordinary
            positional and named arguments and declared defaults all work.

            The accepted syntax includes choice field defaults. The Alpha compiler
            still needs to implement them. See @../choices/#variant-construction (Variant construction).
        ]

        [routes_basic]
    ]
]

[section:
    [doc_level:
        [$in key:wiring-scope-and-future]
        [$in title:Reading, forwarding and handling a message]
        [$in advanced, $md:
            A child editor can describe a change with its own choice type. A
            parent with several editors also needs to know which one changed.
            It can capture the editor ID in a parent constructor and leave room
            for one complete child message.

            [codeblock, $code "moth":
                EditorMessage = {
                    Changed | text String |,
                }

                AppMessage = {
                    Editor | id I32, message EditorMessage |,
                }

                editor_label |on_change Route of EditorMessage -> AppMessage| -> String {
                    return "Editor"
                }

                toolbar |on_change Route of EditorMessage -> AppMessage| -> String {
                    return editor_label(on_change)
                }

                io.line(toolbar(AppMessage::Editor(7)))
            ]

            This prints `Editor`. The caller forms one Route, and `toolbar`
            forwards it unchanged. A consumer with a child message could use this
            constructor to identify editor 7. Handling a real edit would require
            a separate consumer to apply the Route.

            The JavaScript/HTML target handles this forwarding. The HTML-Wasm
            target supports Wires and rejects reachable Route formation. A UI
            builder would still need to define event delivery, observation and
            how long it can retain a binding. An ordinary String template can
            display the text produced by one call. It establishes no subscription.
            Channels have a separate, as yet unimplemented, transport contract.
        ]

        [scope_and_future_basic]
    ]
]

